dsi_dev[sel]->dsi_vid_ctl0.bits.vact_lp_en = 1;
```

These LP (Low Power) enables during VSA, VBP, VACT are suspicious. During VACT (active video), the DSI should NOT be in LP mode! LP mode is for blanking periods only.

Setting `vact_lp_en = 1` means the DSI controller transitions to LP mode during the active video period... wait, no, that can't be right. Let me re-read.

Actually, in DSI terminology:
- `vsa_lp_en`: VSA (Vertical Sync Active) in LP mode
- `vbp_lp_en`: VBP (Vertical Back Porch) in LP mode
- `vact_lp_en`: VACT (Vertical Active) in LP mode
- `vfp_lp_en`: VFP (Vertical Front Porch) in LP mode

Setting `vact_lp_en = 1` would be VERY wrong because the active video lines must be in HS (High Speed) mode, not LP mode. If the DSI transitions to LP mode during active video, the panel won't receive pixel data during those lines!

But this applies to ALL DSI panels and they all seem to work... so maybe `vact_lp_en` means something different. Or maybe the LP mode transition only happens at the end of VACT (after all active lines), effectively acting as a VFP.

Let me read the DSI 28nm driver more carefully to understand the context.

